<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="introduction" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Introduction</title>

  <p>
    In this document, we present a framework that can help any afterschool program in developing
    culturally responsive teaching (CRT) in a sustained and coherent way, and that can keep track
    of progress made in the different components that comprise CRT, at different levels. This is
    far from a straightforward task, as it involves multiple efforts in different directions,
    constant reflection, and aligning all efforts. We hope that this document serves as a useful
    and practical guide to start this exciting process.
  </p>

  <p>
    Our program began thinking about these dimensions of culturally responsive teaching to help
    understand how to categorize the ways we were adapting the program to culturally responsive
    practices. We wanted to develop a better knowledge of why certain practices are helpful and
    others are less so, and ways to explain the key important ideas that can be followed at
    different levels (leadership teams, curriculum development, training and coaching, mentoring
    observation, and assessment). Over time, we listed practices that we found beneficial, began
    to foster them, and saw which practices had similar goals or outcomes. This resulted in
    constructing the 6 Dimensions, which gives a general, coherent structure for the practices
    which we found were most impactful for students, and needed to be sustained at different levels.
  </p>
</section>
